docs(design): BRAND.md as canonical palette + vitest contract pins to tokens.semantic.css

Closing the recurring 'agent cites retired brand palette' bug at
the source. The 2026-05-22 trigger was me citing 'cinnabar/sandstone
Harbor Heritage' from a 2026-03-26 memory snapshot, two months
after the live palette had moved to cobalt/teal/cream. The user
called it out and asked for a durable fix.

Two-layer fix:

1. **Memory layer** (already shipped, this branch only references
   it): a Critical-DNA rule says 'never quote hex values, palette
   names, type sizes, or spacing values in memory; point at the
   source-of-truth file.' Rule lives at
   ~/.claude/.../feedback_no_inline_design_specifics.md and is
   hoisted to the top of MEMORY.md.

2. **Repo layer** (this PR): website-v2/docs/design/BRAND.md is
   now the canonical human-readable palette doc — what blog hero
   prompt engineers, OG-card generators, and outside designers
   read. Naming the palette (cobalt-cream-teal flat editorial,
   maritime signal vocabulary, no skeuomorphism) is anchored
   here, not in private memory.

   A new vitest contract in design-system-contracts.test.ts
   walks every '| `--token` | `#hex` |' table row in
   BRAND.md and asserts the hex matches the corresponding
   declaration in tokens.semantic.css (light and dark blocks
   separately). If a future commit changes the CSS without
   updating BRAND.md (or vice versa), CI fails with a precise
   diff message naming the token + both values.

   Sabotaged the test with #ff0000 to verify it fires; got the
   expected '--brand-primary: BRAND.md says #ff0000,
   tokens.semantic.css light theme has #003fb8' diff. Restored
   and the test passes.

The Forbidden Phrases section in BRAND.md explicitly enumerates
the retired terms ('Harbor Heritage', 'cinnabar red', 'sandstone',
'warm ebony', 'neumorphic') so future authors can quickly tell
they're reading a stale snapshot.

Validation:
  - vitest: contract passes; sabotage test fires correctly
  - tsc --noEmit clean


